{:edb/db-before-t 1939 :edb/db-after-t 1940 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535252 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:27:21.333Z" 13194139535252 true] [17592186179959 1100 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise." 13194139535252 false] [17592186179959 1100 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise. Interpret circulation as the sum of four convergent improper line integrals over the open quadrant arcs, with independent one-sided limits at $(\\pm1,0)$ and $(0,\\pm5)$. The field is undefined at those four points, so the original ordinary circulation is not defined." 13194139535252 true] [17592186179959 1145 "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+5 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535252 false] [17592186179959 1145 "The original field is undefined at the four axis intersections, so an ordinary line integral over the whole ellipse is not defined. For the explicit improper interpretation, $r(t)=(\\cos t,5\\sin t)$ traverses $25x^2+y^2=25$ counterclockwise. On each open quadrant arc,\n\n$$\\frac{5}{y}\\,dx=\\frac{5}{5\\sin t}(-\\sin t)\\,dt=-dt,\\qquad\\frac{1}{x}\\,dy=\\frac{1}{\\cos t}(5\\cos t)\\,dt=5dt.$$\n\nThus the scalar pullback is $4$. Each endpoint has the finite one-sided limit $4$, so each open-arc integral converges absolutely and equals $2\\pi$. Their sum is $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{imp}}=8\\pi$, the unchanged option c. No principal-value cancellation is used, and $F$ remains undefined at the excluded points." 13194139535252 true]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
